%[text] # Feedforward + Feedback: Block Diagram Algebra in MATLAB
%[text] Companion to the note *Feedforward + Feedback: Block diagrams and steady-state error* (feedforward\_blockdiagrams\_sse.pdf). We reduce the feedforward + feedback loop to a single equivalent transfer function, first by hand and then three ways in MATLAB, and check that a feedforward gain $F = 1/K\_{dc}$ removes the steady-state error.
%[text] ![Feedforward + feedback block diagram](text:image:a1f0)
%[text] ## Equivalent transfer function by hand
%[text] Write one equation per summing junction and block:
%[text] $E = R - Y, \\qquad U\_{fb} = C\\,E, \\qquad U = U\_{fb} + F\\,R, \\qquad Y = G\\,U$
%[text] Substitute and collect the $Y$ terms, $Y\\left\[1 + GC\\right\] = G\\left\[C + F\\right\]R$, which gives
%[text] $T(s) = \\frac{Y(s)}{R(s)} = \\frac{G(s)\\left\[C(s) + F(s)\\right\]}{1 + G(s)C(s)}$
%[text] The denominator is the same as for feedback alone, so feedforward does not move the closed-loop poles. Feedforward only changes the numerator.
%%
%[text] ## Define the blocks
%[text] USV surge plant (first order), a proportional feedback controller and a constant feedforward gain equal to the inverse of the plant DC gain.
%[text] **Plant:** USV model transfer function - first order
%[text] $G(s) = \\frac{K\_{dc}} {\\tau \\, s + 1} = \\frac{2.2}{1.5 \\, s + 1$
K_dc = 2.2;                  
tau = 1.5;                
G = tf(K_dc, [tau 1])
%[text] Feedback Controller: Proportional-only control;
%[text] $C(s) = K\_p = 0.7$
KP = 0.7;
C = tf(KP);
%[text] Feedforward Gain: Constant gain, set at the reciprical of the plant's DC gain.
%[text] $F(s) = K\_{ff} = 1/K\_{dc}$
F = tf(1/K_dc)               %
%%
%[text] ## Method 1: Type in the formula
%[text] Transfer-function arithmetic works directly, but MATLAB does not cancel common factors on its own. 
T_raw = G*(C + F)/(1 + G*C)
%[text] It is not obvious from the output, but a consequence of doing the block diagram algebra this way is that there can be identical poles and zeros that mathematically cancel out, but MATLAB doesn't catch.   We can see this looking at the poles and zeros and see there is both a pole and a zero at $s = -1/\\tau = -0.6667$
[p,z] = pzmap(T_raw)
%[text] Luckily, the MATLAB `minreal` command does the job of canceling these pole-zero pairs, so we can simplify that effective transfer function 
T1 = minreal(T_raw)
 
 
%[text] Notice that the effective transfer function, including both feedback and feedforward terms, is still a first-order system.  Also notice that the DC-gain of this transfer function is unity, which tells us that the steady-state error for a step input will be zero, because the input and output will have the same magnitude at steady-state: steady-state output = steady-state input \* DC gain
%%
%[text] ## Method 2: Adapt the `feedback` command. 
%[text] `feedback(G, C)` is the loop with $G$ in the forward path and $C$ in the return path, $G/(1 + GC)$. 
%[text] Multiplying that by by $(C + F)$ gives ...
%[text] $T = \\frac{G}{1 + GC} \\cdot \\left\[C + F\\right\]$
T2 = feedback(G, C) * (C + F)
%[text] Not that the simplification, canceling any poles and zeros, is done as part of the `feedback` call. 
%%
%[text] ## Check: Both agree
%[text] Compare pole-zero-gain forms.
zpk(T1)
zpk(T2)
%%
%[text] ## Feedforward removes the steady-state error
%[text] Feedback alone has DC gain $K\_P K\_{dc}/(1 + K\_P K\_{dc}) \< 1$. With $F = 1/K\_{dc}$ the DC gain is exactly 1.
T_fb = feedback(G*C, 1);     % feedback only (F = 0)

dcgain_fb   = dcgain(T_fb)
dcgain_ffwd = dcgain(T1)
%[text] The closed-loop poles are the same with and without feedforward:
pole(T_fb)
pole(T1)
%%
%[text] ## Step response
step(T_fb, T1, 10)
legend('Feedback only', 'Feedforward + feedback', 'Location', 'southeast')
grid on
%[text] Both responses have the same time constant (same pole), but only the feedforward loop settles on the setpoint.

